From b3f6b7ec1d98183b5450990421bd911c95c7cb4b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 15:48:47 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 15/17] nvme-pci: acquire the dma-buf active reference at
 hardware submission

Wire nvme into map->active, taken and dropped at the actual hardware
boundary rather than at io_uring import time -- the point of the
kref/active split earlier in this series. nvme_iod::flags is widened
from u8 to u16 in the same commit that adds the new IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE
bit (1U << 8), with an IOD_FLAGS_LAST sentinel and a BUILD_BUG_ON in
nvme_dmabuf_tryget_active() so the field width is checked at compile
time from now on. A too-narrow field silently truncates the |=, which
would make nvme_dmabuf_put_active() a permanent no-op, leak every active
reference, and hang invalidation forever with no diagnostic.

Acquire: first statement of nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf(), before
nvme_pci_dmabuf_sgl_nents() or either setup helper reads any driver DMA
state (map->sgt, map->dma_list, ...). Everything downstream of this
point is already GFP_ATOMIC (dma_pool_alloc() for PRP/SGL descriptors),
so the reference's held window contains no reclaim-capable allocation --
this is the whole point of moving the boundary here instead of leaving
it at import.

Release: last statement of nvme_rq_clean_dmabuf_map(), after
nvme_dmabuf_map_sync_for_cpu() (which still needs a live mapping).

Enforce the boundary with the IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE flag rather than trust
call-site discipline:

  - nvme_req_dmabuf_map() is now the only accessor to driver DMA state
    outside the unmap callback, and WARN_ON_ONCE()s if the flag isn't
    set. All read sites (both sync helpers, both setup helpers, the SGL
    nents counter) go through it instead of touching bio->bi_dmabuf_map
    directly.
  - nvme_dmabuf_put_active() is a no-op unless the flag is set, so it is
    safe to call unconditionally from any exit path -- double-puts
    become structurally impossible rather than something to audit for.
  - nvme_prep_rq() WARN_ON_ONCE()s the flag is clear *before* its
    `iod->flags = 0` reset. That reset runs on every requeue; without the
    assertion ahead of it, a leaked reference from a prior pass would be
    silently erased before anything could catch it.

nvme_prep_rq()'s failure path (out_free_cmd) skips nvme_unmap_data()
entirely, so nvme_rq_clean_dmabuf_map() does not run for a request that
fails setup. Every such exit inside nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_map() and
nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_sgl() now calls nvme_dmabuf_put_active() locally
before returning, so the reference is always balanced regardless of
which path a request takes.

A tryget failure at the funnel (map generation invalidated by exporter
migration) returns NVME_STS_DMABUF_GENERATION_LOST, a documented
BLK_STS_IOERR placeholder -- not BLK_STS_AGAIN, which
include/linux/blk_types.h reserves for RQF_NOWAIT requests whose bio
would block, which this is not. Choosing BLK_STS_IOERR also means
io_uring's transparent reissue never triggers for this failure:
io_rw_should_reissue() and REQ_F_REISSUE are both gated on -EAGAIN (or
-EOPNOTSUPP for the non-IOPOLL completion path), neither of which -EIO
satisfies. This matters because one direct I/O can produce several
requests, and a generation loss between their nvme_queue_rq() calls
would otherwise let io_uring reissue a range that already completed
against the old mapping -- unsafe duplicate I/O under concurrent writers
or on zoned namespaces, not merely redundant. -EIO is not the right
errno for a recoverable, retryable condition; it is the smallest change
for this prototype. A dedicated status (mapping to something like
-ESTALE) is a block/uAPI decision that needs sign-off before this is
upstreamable, and userspace still needs telling that a generation-loss
completion may cover a request that was only partially submitted.

This commit also completes the boundary move begun by the previous one:
dma_buf_io_get_map() / dma_buf_io_create_map() (io_uring's import path)
no longer take a reference on ->active at all -- only nvme's submission
funnel above does, matching the header's ->active contract (acquired
only at hardware submission, never at import). The import-time
percpu_ref_get()/tryget() is removed in the same diff that adds the
submission-time one, so there is no intermediate state where both are
held.

The next commit fixes nvme_prep_rq_batch()'s status handling, which
currently collapses this terminal failure into an indefinite requeue
loop on the nvme_queue_rqs() batch path.

Signed-off-by: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>

